<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agustín de Iturbide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Agustín I of Mexico, Emperor Agustín I, Agustín I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agustín de Iturbide was a Mexican royalist officer who changed sides in 1821 and, with the insurgent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In May 1822 he was proclaimed Emperor Agustín I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>, but opposition from congress and the army forced him to abdicate in March 1823. Returning from exile in 1824, he was arrested and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide.jpg" alt="Agustín de Iturbide" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Primitivo Miranda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agustin_I_of_Mexico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Agustín Cosme Damián de Iturbide y Arámburu was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, now Morelia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> on 27 September 1783, the fifth child of his parents and the only male to survive. His father, Joaquín de Iturbide, came from a Basque family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> as a younger son to make his fortune. The family were part of the landed class of Valladolid. In 1805 Iturbide entered the royal Spanish army and married Ana María Josefa Ramona de Huarte y Muñiz, whose dowry of 100,000 pesos bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">Hacienda</a> of San José de Apeo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the War of Independence broke out in 1810, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a> offered Iturbide the rank of lieutenant general in the insurgent forces, and he refused. He compiled a record of victories, often against far larger numbers, and earned the name El Dragón de Hierro, the Iron Dragon, but contemporaries of all factions also knew him for cruelty towards insurgents and their families. He was dismissed from command in 1816 over accusations of corruption and cruelty, and was reinstated in 1820 to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>.</p><p>The restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s liberal Cadiz Constitution in 1820 made many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> fear that the monarchy would be forced to abandon Spain. Iturbide allied with the insurgents under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a> of 1821, which aimed at an independent monarchy with Roman Catholicism as its official religion and the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a>, signed with the Spanish official O'Donojú, offered the throne to Ferdinand VII or another European prince; when Spain refused, Iturbide entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 27 September 1821 and became president of the Regency.</p><p>He took the crown as emperor in 1822. Conflict with Congress, which claimed all the powers of the state, led him to dissolve it on 31 October 1822. A military revolt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalupe-victoria">Guadalupe Victoria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> followed in December, and he abdicated on 19 March 1823.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Iturbide went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in May 1823. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on 14 July 1824, landing at Soto la Marina, and was arrested by General Felipe de la Garza. He was executed by a local legislature on 19 July 1824. Early in the independence period the date of independence depended on politics: conservatives favoured 27 September, when Iturbide entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and liberals 16 September, Hidalgo's call to rebellion. Writing about him in modern Mexico is often hostile. The historian Eric Van Young calls him an opportunist who showed moments of political brilliance clouded by bad judgment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agustín de Iturbide” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weak and divided, most of the provinces accepted annexation to <b>Agustín de Iturbide</b>’s First Mexican Empire in January 1822; San Salvador resisted until a Mexican army under Vicente Filísola occupied it in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After liberal revolution in Spain in 1820, the royalist officer <b>Agustín de Iturbide</b> agreed with Guerrero on the Plan of Iguala of February 1821, promising independence, Catholicism and equality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After independence from Spain in 1821 it resisted annexation to <b>Agustín de Iturbide</b>’s Mexican Empire, its congress even voting in 1822 to seek union with the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After independence from Spain in 1821 and a brief annexation to <b>Agustín de Iturbide</b>’s Mexican Empire, Guatemala became the largest and most populous state of the Federal Republic of Central America, whose government sat in Guatemala City until 1834.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicaragua emerged from the break-up of the Federal Republic of Central America, the union formed by the former provinces of the Captaincy General of Guatemala after independence from Spain in 1821 and from <b>Agustín de Iturbide</b>’s Mexican Empire in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Agustín de Iturbide</b> and Vicente Guerrero proclaimed a plan for an independent Mexican monarchy guaranteeing Catholicism and equality.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Morelia, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Nueva Villa de Padilla, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.7,-101.19&amp;year=1809" aria-label="Morelia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1818 1434 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Agustín de Iturbide"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2268 1704h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2268 1704h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.7,-101.19&amp;year=1809">What was here in 1809? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
